From: Lycaos <caraboune@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:58:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] pager-test-drift: refresh settings tests the privacy patches
 invalidated
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Nine tests in the settings suite were red before this patch, and none of
them ran anywhere: the privacy contracts only invoke the targets they
declare, and `cargo test -p xai-grok-pager` is not one of them.

Six were drift from this fork's own patches.

`retention-opt-out` relabels the coding-data choice "Opt out (locked)" and
reduces the catalog to that single entry. The row-render control arm
distinguished locked from unlocked by the absence of the word "locked",
which no longer separates them — the account lock now shows as the
` · Admin Managed` / `ZDR` suffix, so that is what it compares. Two picker
tests assumed a second choice to navigate to; the picker still opens on an
unlocked snapshot, so the replacement pins what actually holds: no key
sequence in it can commit an opt-in.

`vendor-updater-hard-off` flips `auto_update` off by default, so its two
toggle tests land on `true`, and the `move_setting_away_from_default` helper
had to follow.

`search("privacy")` was pinned to exactly one hit; this fork has three
legitimate ones (retention, session sync, and auto-update, whose description
explains why vendor updates are off). The upstream comment prescribed a
presence check for exactly this case.

Three were not defects at all. The modal reads `combine_queued_prompts` and
`collapsed_edit_blocks` from the appearance cache, which seeds from the
user's own ~/.grok/config.toml, while the tests derived their expectation
from `UiConfig::default()`. They pass on a clean machine and fail for any
developer who set those two. Pinned through the cache setters, the way the
same test already pins five other keys.

Gork-Patch-Id: pager-test-drift
Gork-Invariant: settings-suite-green
Gork-Risk: low
